 <p>As well as unlimited notes on individual customers, webERP has the functionality to store an unlimited number of notes on groups of customers based on the debtor type field. As with the notes on individual customers , these notes are displayed in order of priority, and can contain a URL as well as the free form note.</p>
 <!-- Help End: Customers -->
+<!-- Help Begin: CreditControl -->
+<h2><a id="CreditControl">Credit Control</a></h2>
+
+<p>Credit control is done by the credit controller, the deputy and the Finance Director. They need the security token <i>Credit control</i> (21). The approval of large write-offs needs the token <i>approval of large bad debt write-offs</i> (22), given to the Finance Director. Sales reps and sales office staff can see credit information, but cannot change a credit status or a credit limit.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditSettings">Settings</a></h3>
+
+<p>Finance sets the rules in Accounts Receivable, Maintenance, <i>Credit Control Settings</i>: the standard review period in months, whether orders over the credit limit are held, the days between reminder letters, whether the final letter puts the customer on stop, the interest rate, the late fee, the days overdue before charges are made, the general ledger accounts for interest, fees and bad debts, the write-off amount above which the Finance Director must approve, and whether a write-off puts the customer on stop.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CustomerCredit">Credit reviews, stop and watch</a></h3>
+
+<p>From the Select Customer screen choose <i>Credit Status, Reviews and Reminders</i>. Credit control can:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><b>Record a review</b> - the outcome (limit confirmed, raised, reduced, put on watch, put on stop), the new limit, the notes and the next review date. A new limit applies at once, in the same step as the review that explains it. Reviews are kept for ever and cannot be changed or deleted.</li>
+<li><b>Change the credit status</b> with a reason. Who, when and why are kept in the credit control history.</li>
+<li><b>Exempt the customer from interest and/or late fees</b>, with a reason.</li>
+<li><b>Mark invoices as disputed</b>. A disputed invoice is not chased and does not attract interest until the dispute is settled.</li>
+<li><b>Add a note</b>, for example that the debt has been passed to a collection agency or to lawyers.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>New customers get a next review date automatically, one standard review period after the account is opened. Existing customers were given a date when the system was upgraded.</p>
+
+<p>A customer <b>on stop</b> cannot be given a new order by any route - the sales office, the web shop, EDI or the recurring orders - and goods are not dispatched or invoiced on orders taken before the stop, unless credit control releases the order in <i>Credit Held and Watched Orders</i>. A customer <b>on watch</b> can still be supplied. Whoever takes the order is warned and the orders show on the credit control list. The status is shown wherever staff look at the customer. A customer sees only that the account is on hold, never the watch status.</p>
+
+<p>When an order takes a customer over their credit limit the order is accepted but <b>held</b>. It cannot be dispatched or invoiced until credit control releases it in <i>Credit Held and Watched Orders</i>.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="DunningRun">The overdue reminder run</a></h3>
+
+<p>The wording and timing of the letters are kept in <i>Reminder Letter Stages</i>: the number of stages, the days overdue for each and the subject and wording. The wording may use {name}, {debtorno}, {currency}, {amount}, {invoices}, {company} and {date}. Three stages are supplied: a polite reminder, a firmer letter and a final notice.</p>
+
+<p>Credit control starts the run, normally once a week, from <i>Overdue Reminder Run</i>. The run lists who would get which letter. Untick the customers to leave out, for example where a payment is promised, then send. Letters are emailed to the contacts of the customer that receive statements and are recorded against the customer. Customers with no email address are listed so that the letter can be posted. A customer moves up one stage at a time and only while an undisputed invoice is still overdue by the days of the next stage. A customer who pays between the list and the send gets nothing, and a customer is not sent the same stage twice, nor two letters within the minimum days set in the settings. Customers see their reminder letters in the portal.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditCharges">Month-end interest and late fees</a></h3>
+
+<p>After a month ends, <i>Month-End Interest and Fees</i> lists what would be charged to whom. Interest is a simple annual rate on undisputed invoices overdue by at least the days set in the settings at the end of the month. The late fee is a fixed amount in the home currency, converted to the currency of the customer, charged once per customer per month. Interest and fees are not charged on interest and fees, and exempt customers are not charged. Confirming the list posts a separate, clearly described interest item and fee item to each customer account and to the general ledger income accounts chosen by Finance. They show on the next statement and in the portal. The same month cannot be charged twice. A charge can be waived, with a reason, if it has not been paid. The waiver is also posted to the ledger.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="BadDebtWriteOff">Bad debt write-off</a></h3>
+
+<p>From <i>Bad Debt Write-off</i> credit control writes off all or part of an unpaid balance with a reason. A write-off up to the approval limit is posted at once. A larger one waits for the Finance Director to approve it and does nothing to the account until then. A posted write-off credits the customer account, is allocated to the oldest unpaid invoices and is debited to the bad debt account, with any exchange difference posted as for an allocation. Unless credit control decides otherwise the customer is put on stop. The <i>Bad Debt Write-off Report</i> shows the write-offs over a period with who requested and who approved each one. If a written-off debt is later paid, enter the receipt as an unallocated receipt and use the bad debt recovery process agreed with the Financial Accountant.</p>
+
+<h3><a id="CreditControlReport">Reports</a></h3>
+
+<ul>
+<li><b>Credit Control Report</b> - customers due or overdue for a credit review, customers over their credit limit and by how much, and customers on watch or on stop. It can be filtered by sales rep and by currency and totals are in the home currency. A sales rep sees only their own customers.</li>
+<li><b>Aged Customer Balances</b> now also shows each customer's credit limit, credit status and the stage of the last reminder sent.</li>
+<li><b>Debtors Ledger Reconciliation</b> - the total of the customer balances against the trade debtors control account, and what explains a difference.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>For sales staff</h3>
+
+<p><b>Watch</b> means that you may take orders for the customer but you will be warned and credit control will see the order. <b>Stop</b> means that no order can be entered or invoiced for the customer. Tell the customer to contact credit control and do not try to get around it - the order will not be accepted. Over-limit orders are held until credit control releases them. You can see the status and the reason for it, but you cannot change a status or a limit.</p>
+<!-- Help End: CreditControl -->
 <!-- Help Begin: Shippers -->
 <h2>Shippers</h2>
 
diff --git a/includes/CreditControlFunctions.php b/includes/CreditControlFunctions.php
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6ad1d79
